From ee43cd11182a278c952b831fecb66f41586b53eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 13:10:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 12/15] daemon: hold a pool of RPC connections rather than one

`jsonrpc`'s transport keeps a single socket behind a mutex, so every call on a
`Client` waits for the one before it. On a single-threaded server that costs
nothing and is invisible. It is also the ceiling on anything that tries to
overlap: asking for four pruned blocks at once on a shared client takes exactly
as long as asking for them one after another, so concurrency added anywhere
above this layer would have bought nothing.

Four connections, each with its own socket, handed out per call and returned on
drop. The size is deliberate rather than generous: a slot in use can become peer
connections opened by the proxy in front of a pruned node, and a node has only
so many peers worth asking, so the throttle belongs here where the work is
issued rather than downstream where it would be discovered.

The connection that ran the startup checks becomes the first of the pool, so the
node that answered them is the node the pool talks to.

This is groundwork: nothing yet issues concurrent calls. What it removes is the
reason doing so would not have helped.
